  "worked_solution": "Let's go through each of the options.\n\n- The function $f(y)$ is homogeneous of degree $2.$ It is a polynomial of degree $2,$ with no linear or constant term.\n\n- The function $g(x,y)$ is not homogeneous. It is a polynomial of degree $5.$ However, it also contains a term ($2{x}^{3}y$) that is of degree $4.$ Therefore, not all of its terms are of degree $5.$\n\n- The function $h(x,y,z)$ is homogeneous of degree $3.$ It is a polynomial of degree $3,$ and all of its terms are of degree $3.$",
